# UAC Import Surface Enforcement

## Rule

All consumer repos MUST import from UAC facade modules only:

```python
# ALLOWED — facade imports (domain-level)
from unified_api_contracts.execution import CanonicalOrder, CanonicalFill
from unified_api_contracts.instruments import CanonicalInstrument
from unified_api_contracts.market_data import CanonicalCandle, CanonicalTick
from unified_api_contracts.portfolio import CanonicalPosition, CanonicalBalance
from unified_api_contracts.risk import CanonicalRiskMetrics
from unified_api_contracts.features import CanonicalFeatureRow
from unified_api_contracts.normalize import normalize_order, normalize_fill
```

## Blocked Patterns

```python
# BLOCKED — deep/internal imports
from unified_api_contracts.canonical.execution import ...     # deep canonical
from unified_api_contracts.normalize_utils.order import ...   # deep normalize
from unified_api_contracts.config.venues import ...           # internal config
from unified_api_contracts.shared.enums import ...            # internal shared
from unified_api_contracts.schemas.v2 import ...              # internal schemas
```

## Exempt Repos

These repos set `UAC_CANONICAL_EXEMPT=true` in their `quality-gates.sh`:

- `unified-api-contracts` — UAC itself (defines the internals)
- `unified-internal-contracts` — UIC (sibling schema repo)
- `system-integration-tests` — SIT (tests internal structure)

## QG Enforcement

STEP 5.23 in `base-service.sh` and `base-library.sh` blocks deep UAC imports.
Bypass: set `UAC_CANONICAL_EXEMPT=true` before sourcing the base script.

## Why

The facade pattern ensures UAC can refactor internal module layout (canonical/, shared/, config/)
without breaking consumers. All public symbols are re-exported through domain facade modules.
